## 8. Explicit gaps and non-sources

The two licensed sources do **not** cleanly provide the following, so this
document does not invent them:

1. The 2014 DMG's Easy/Medium/Hard/Deadly XP thresholds or its creature-count
   multipliers. They do not appear here.
2. An official offensive-CR/defensive-CR procedure for deriving CR from AC, HP,
   accuracy, DPR, save DC, or special features. The LGMRD quick table is an
   attributed third-party benchmark, not an official CR calculator.
3. A daily adventuring XP budget, prescribed encounter count per day, or rest
   schedule for encounter building.
4. A licensed numeric conversion from Low/Moderate/High to expected rounds,
   resource-spend percentages, knockout chance, death chance, or win chance.
   Section 6's round and fractional-resource gates are explicitly original
   project calibration.
5. A three-character hard-combination table in the LGMRD. Its combination
   tables cover four, five, and six characters; the three-PC case uses the SRD
   budget and Lazy benchmark only.
6. A universal one-hit minion rule, a precise boss action-economy adjustment, or
   a numeric CR cost for monster roles.
7. A prescribed XP adjustment for terrain, cover, hazards, surprise, unusual
   objectives, or favorable positioning. Those factors are warnings and design
   inputs, then simulation inputs where modeled.
8. A reconciliation rule for disagreements among the SRD budget, the LGMRD
   hard-combination table, and the Lazy benchmark. The level-7 six-CR-2 example
   demonstrates that disagreement; simulation evidence and owner approval are
   the project resolution.
9. Enough CR in the current 4–6-creature starter roster to satisfy every
   requested pressure. Moderate and High for four PCs and High for three PCs are
   impossible under the proposed non-overlapping XP bands.
10. D317.9's full local decision text. Only the owner-supplied
    `{ rounds, pressure }` meaning is available in this lane.
11. A current typed storage/provenance model for homebrew CR above 3 or for
    owner-authored detailed statblocks, nor general typed actions for save
    effects, recharge abilities, or legendary actions. Section 5 identifies the
    required type replacement but does not specify a persistence schema.
# Encounter and Monster Design Ideas Digest

This clean-room digest abstracts methods from publicly readable sources. It intentionally omits source prose, source tables, numeric benchmarks, stat-block text, and the sources' original presentation order.

## Encounter math

- **EM-001** — `encounter-math` — Treat a difficulty calculation as an early-warning device, not a promise about the result. Use it mainly to detect fights that may be unexpectedly lethal. Source: https://slyflourish.com/5e_encounter_building.html
- **EM-002** — `encounter-math` — Choose opponents that make sense in the fiction before applying a balance check. If the check exposes excessive danger, change the situation or make retreat and failure survivable rather than forcing the fiction to obey a budget. Source: https://slyflourish.com/5e_encounter_building.html
- **EM-003** — `encounter-math` — Calibrate any generic benchmark against the actual group after observing play. Coordination, character options, player experience, equipment, and current resource depletion can all shift practical difficulty. Source: https://slyflourish.com/5e_encounter_building.html
- **EM-004** — `encounter-math` — Evaluate enemy quantity separately from individual rating because additional bodies amplify pressure through targeting, positioning, and extra opportunities to act. A collection of weak foes is not equivalent to a lone creature with the same nominal budget. Source: https://slyflourish.com/5e_encounter_building.html
- **EM-005** — `encounter-math` — Avoid making every battle land on the same difficulty band. Contrasting easy victories, uncertain contests, and clearly overwhelming threats gives the campaign texture and makes danger legible. Source: https://slyflourish.com/5e_encounter_building.html
- **EM-006** — `encounter-math` — When approximate balance may fail, prepare consequences that keep play moving after defeat. Retreat routes, capture, lost objectives, or changed circumstances can make a dangerous result meaningful without ending the campaign. Source: https://slyflourish.com/5e_encounter_building.html
- **EM-007** — `encounter-math` — Judge a creature's threat by how much harm it can inflict before the party neutralizes it, rather than by durability or output alone. Defensive and offensive changes interact multiplicatively because surviving longer creates more chances to deal damage. Source: https://tomedunn.github.io/the-finished-book/monsters/monster-manual-2024/
- **EM-008** — `encounter-math` — Be cautious when inferring a benchmark from sparse high-tier examples. Prefer a broad trend and reserve extreme values for deliberately exceptional designs instead of treating a lone outlier as the new norm. Source: https://www.blogofholding.com/?p=8469

## Action economy

- **AE-001** — `action-economy` — Compare how often each side can materially affect the battle, not merely how much damage its best attack deals. A side with more useful turns can reposition, recover from misses, apply control, and finish weakened targets more reliably. Source: https://slyflourish.com/5e_encounter_building.html
- **AE-002** — `action-economy` — Give a solo opponent meaningful activity between player turns so the battlefield responds throughout the round. Off-turn movement, attacks, or control are more engaging than concentrating all of the boss's impact into one turn. Source: https://slyflourish.com/action_oriented_monsters.html
- **AE-003** — `action-economy` — An alternative solo chassis can model several creatures sharing one body. Its available turns and endurance then reflect how many functional components remain, allowing attrition to reduce its battlefield presence. Source: https://theangrygm.com/return-of-the-son-of-the-dd-boss-fight-now-in-5e/
- **AE-004** — `action-economy` — Spread a boss's extra activity across the initiative order instead of granting a single oversized turn. This creates more frequent decision points and reduces long stretches in which the encounter cannot react. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/minions_elites_solos.html
- **AE-005** — `action-economy` — Minions can restore numerical pressure without giving every added body the endurance and complexity of a standard foe. Their job is to consume attention, occupy space, and create openings for more important enemies. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/minions_elites_solos.html
- **AE-006** — `action-economy` — Reinforcements are a flexible difficulty control because they alter enemy activity only if the encounter needs it. Their arrival should follow a visible fictional cause such as an alarm, summons, or nearby guards. Source: https://slyflourish.com/lazy_5e_monster_building_resource_document.html
- **AE-007** — `action-economy` — Faster initiative improves a monster's realized output because acting before opponents can remove or disable it yields more completed actions. Treat initiative capability as part of combat power, especially for elite threats. Source: https://tomedunn.github.io/the-finished-book/monsters/monster-manual-2024/

## Monster stats

- **MS-001** — `monster-stats` — Begin with the creature's intended threat and battlefield behavior, then choose statistics that enable that behavior. Starting from a pile of numbers makes it easy to produce a legal but purposeless opponent. Source: https://theangrygm.com/monster-building-202/
- **MS-002** — `monster-stats` — Separate offensive and defensive evaluations so a design can intentionally trade staying power for pressure, or the reverse. The combined result may remain suitable even when the creature has a pronounced strength and weakness. Source: https://theangrygm.com/monster-building-202/
- **MS-003** — `monster-stats` — Count always-on traits and reliable special abilities as part of the relevant stat budget. An effect that routinely improves accuracy, avoidance, durability, or output is not free merely because it is written as prose. Source: https://theangrygm.com/monster-building-202/
- **MS-004** — `monster-stats` — Use baseline statistics as a starting envelope, then tune through play for the specific party. Published creatures at the same nominal threat can vary, and unusual effects are difficult to reduce to a single rating. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/making_monsters.html
- **MS-005** — `monster-stats` — Give each monster dependable ordinary attacks before layering on role-specific powers. A clear baseline keeps the creature functional when its signature move is unavailable and prevents every turn from requiring an exceptional rule. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/making_monsters.html
- **MS-006** — `monster-stats` — Recent monster data suggests accuracy and basic defenses remain comparatively stable while damage growth becomes steeper at upper tiers. Updating older designs therefore calls for special scrutiny of output rather than uniform inflation of every statistic. Source: https://tomedunn.github.io/the-finished-book/monsters/monster-manual-2024/
- **MS-007** — `monster-stats` — For a glass-cannon or tank identity, pair an above-baseline value with a meaningful weakness instead of raising every major statistic together. Data-driven baselines are most useful as a center around which deliberate variation occurs. Source: https://www.blogofholding.com/?p=8469
- **MS-008** — `monster-stats` — Prefer transparent durability over defenses that work only against parties lacking a particular damage solution. Fewer conditional physical resistances make the same creature perform more consistently across groups with different equipment. Source: https://tomedunn.github.io/the-finished-book/monsters/monster-manual-2024/

## Roles

- **RL-001** — `roles` — Define a monster by the job it performs during play, such as holding a line, attacking from safety, disrupting formation, or exploiting exposed targets. The role should tell the GM what to attempt on the creature's turn. Source: https://theangrygm.com/monster-building-202/
- **RL-002** — `roles` — Make mechanics reinforce the declared job: protectors need tools that keep allies safe, ambushers need access and escape, and ranged threats need ways to preserve distance. Labels without enabling rules do not create distinct play. Source: https://theangrygm.com/monster-building-202/
- **RL-003** — `roles` — Combine roles whose behaviors create openings for one another. A durable blocker can shelter a fragile ranged attacker, while forced movement can feed targets into a close-range damage dealer. Source: https://theangrygm.com/monster-building-202/
- **RL-004** — `roles` — Limit the number of different roles in one encounter to what the GM can operate fluently. Tactical variety disappears if cognitive overload causes every creature to fall back on a generic attack. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/building_an_encounter.html
- **RL-005** — `roles` — Create several members of a creature family by combining a shared identity trait with different combat jobs. Players can learn what the family generally does while still having to distinguish its specialists. Source: https://theangrygm.com/monster-building-202/
- **RL-006** — `roles` — Intelligent leaders should select and deploy allies that compensate for their own vulnerabilities or exploit the party's. The minion roster can therefore reveal the leader's planning ability before the leader personally acts. Source: https://www.themonstersknow.com/intelligent-enemy-tactics/

## Bosses and minions

- **BM-001** — `boss-minion` — Build a boss around a short dramatic progression rather than a large spell list. Give it an opening that establishes control, a response to being surrounded, and a desperate escalation that fits its theme. Source: https://slyflourish.com/action_oriented_monsters.html
- **BM-002** — `boss-minion` — Use phase changes to alter decisions, not merely refill endurance. A new form should change movement, target priorities, available cover, hazards, or the boss's role. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/minions_elites_solos.html
- **BM-003** — `boss-minion` — Let a phase boundary clear effects that would otherwise lock the boss out of the whole encounter, but make the transition visible and consistent. This preserves control abilities as useful within a phase without allowing one success to erase the climax. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/minions_elites_solos.html
- **BM-004** — `boss-minion` — A multi-pool boss can shed actions as its components are defeated, producing a natural progress indicator. Players feel their attacks changing the encounter before the final defeat. Source: https://theangrygm.com/return-of-the-son-of-the-dd-boss-fight-now-in-5e/
- **BM-005** — `boss-minion` — Low-endurance lackeys add interference and spectacle while avoiding the prolonged cleanup associated with a room full of full-strength allies. Keep their mechanics uniform enough to operate as a group. Source: https://koboldpress.com/boss-fights-the-duelist/
- **BM-006** — `boss-minion` — Match minions to the boss's fiction and tactical needs instead of adding arbitrary bodies. Suitable followers can screen approaches, pressure vulnerable characters, operate the lair, or cover a weakness. Source: https://www.themonstersknow.com/thoughts-constructing-encounters/
- **BM-007** — `boss-minion` — Legendary opponents in the revised rules appear deliberately more offense-oriented than ordinary creatures at a similar rating. Do not assume several such enemies combine like ordinary peers; their aggregate pressure may exceed the printed encounter signal. Source: https://tomedunn.github.io/the-finished-book/monsters/monster-manual-2024/
- **BM-008** — `boss-minion` — Tie lair activity to the boss's identity and home-field preparation. The environment can give the villain influence across the round without burdening the creature's main action list. Source: https://koboldpress.com/boss-fights-the-duelist/

## Pacing

- **PC-001** — `pacing` — Design minor fights to resolve with less procedural overhead than major set pieces. Save detailed maps, numerous roles, and layered hazards for scenes where those choices justify their time cost. Source: https://theangrygm.com/how-to-f-cr-practical-example-2/
- **PC-002** — `pacing` — Introduce a creature family's basic behavior in an uncomplicated encounter before mixing it with new specialists. Later combinations then test learned understanding rather than presenting an unreadable pile of surprises. Source: https://theangrygm.com/how-to-f-cr-practical-example-2/
- **PC-003** — `pacing` — Plan likely exit behavior for enemies so combat does not automatically continue until every hit point is removed. Surrender, retreat, scattering, or collapse after a leader falls can end a decided fight promptly. Source: https://slyflourish.com/lazy_5e_monster_building_resource_document.html
- **PC-004** — `pacing` — Use waves when a large battle needs changing pressure but placing every enemy at once would overload the table. Staged arrivals also let the GM respond to the party's demonstrated capacity without rewriting existing creatures. Source: https://slyflourish.com/lazy_5e_monster_building_resource_document.html
- **PC-005** — `pacing` — Environmental effects that increase danger to both sides can shorten a stagnant battle while preserving risk. Give players a way to influence or shut down the effect so acceleration remains interactive. Source: https://slyflourish.com/zone_effects.html
- **PC-006** — `pacing` — A climactic encounter earns its status through consequence, novelty, and change, not only through a larger budget. A mathematically ordinary fight can still be a finale if it resolves an important conflict and evolves visibly. Source: https://theangrygm.com/how-to-f-cr-practical-example-2/

## Table running

- **TR-001** — `table-running` — Consolidate closely related attacks and put frequently needed information where the GM can find it at a glance. A shorter decision path helps the creature express more of its design during actual play. Source: https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/1890-preview-the-new-stat-block-design-in-the-2024
- **TR-002** — `table-running` — Replace a broad spell catalogue with a few self-contained thematic actions when the creature is unlikely to need the catalogue's flexibility. This preserves the fantasy while reducing lookup and option paralysis. Source: https://slyflourish.com/action_oriented_monsters.html
- **TR-003** — `table-running` — Use fixed values for routine monster procedures when rolling adds little suspense. Save attention and dice time for decisions that can materially change the scene. Source: https://slyflourish.com/lazy_5e_monster_building_resource_document.html
- **TR-004** — `table-running` — Operate a horde through pooled or averaged procedures while preserving individual figures when positioning matters. Abstraction should remove bookkeeping, not erase player-facing consequences. Source: https://slyflourish.com/running_hordes.html
- **TR-005** — `table-running` — Write a compact tactical script that states the creature's objective, preferred target, opening behavior, fallback, and retreat condition. This supports consistent characterization without requiring perfect optimization during play. Source: https://www.themonstersknow.com/intelligent-enemy-tactics/
- **TR-006** — `table-running` — Let a creature's intelligence govern how quickly it recognizes threats and adapts. Instinctive foes repeat a reliable pattern, while clever foes notice weaknesses, remember prior encounters, and prepare contingencies. Source: https://www.themonstersknow.com/intelligent-enemy-tactics/

## Telegraphing

- **TG-001** — `telegraphing` — Signal an extreme attack before it resolves by describing its source, affected space, and timing. The warning converts unavoidable punishment into a positional problem the players can answer. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/overkill_attacks.html
- **TG-002** — `telegraphing` — Ensure the battlefield contains a feasible response to any announced catastrophe, such as escape routes, usable cover, or a way to interrupt the attacker. A warning without a viable answer is presentation, not agency. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/overkill_attacks.html
- **TG-003** — `telegraphing` — Make charging a devastating move impose a temporary opportunity cost on the monster. Suspending reactions or extra activity gives players a meaningful window to reposition, interfere, or press the attack. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/overkill_attacks.html
- **TG-004** — `telegraphing` — Expose a temporary weak point when a major attack is being prepared. The weak point turns defense into an active team objective and makes specialized attacks or movement valuable. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/overkill_attacks.html
- **TG-005** — `telegraphing` — Reuse recognizable family traits across encounters so players can form reliable expectations, then vary how different specialists apply them. Learned knowledge becomes a tactical reward rather than trivia. Source: https://theangrygm.com/monster-building-202/

## Terrain and objectives

- **TO-001** — `terrain-objectives` — Define what each side wants before deciding that combat ends with annihilation. Goals such as delay, escape, capture, protection, theft, or interruption create different movement and targeting choices. Source: https://www.themonstersknow.com/thoughts-constructing-encounters/
- **TO-002** — `terrain-objectives` — Make the physical location express the defenders' methods. Choke points, observation lines, concealment, escape routes, and hazards should help inhabitants behave as if they chose or prepared the site. Source: https://www.themonstersknow.com/thoughts-constructing-encounters/
- **TO-003** — `terrain-objectives` — Let terrain carry both tactical and narrative information. Architecture, damage, furnishings, and elevation can explain the site's history while simultaneously shaping cover, routes, and control. Source: https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/794-new-players-guide-how-to-build-tactical-encounters
- **TO-004** — `terrain-objectives` — Include at least one meaningful interaction beyond attacking a creature, such as operating a mechanism, moving an object, rescuing someone, or changing a hazard. The interaction should compete with combat actions strongly enough to create a real choice. Source: https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/794-new-players-guide-how-to-build-tactical-encounters
- **TO-005** — `terrain-objectives` — Design win conditions independently from enemy survival. Once the objective is secured or lost, remaining foes may withdraw, pursue a different aim, or become irrelevant. Source: https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/794-new-players-guide-how-to-build-tactical-encounters
- **TO-006** — `terrain-objectives` — Use an active threat or deadline to make task completion more important than clearing the map. This keeps an alternative objective from collapsing back into ordinary attrition. Source: https://slyflourish.com/lazy_5e_monster_building_resource_document.html
- **TO-007** — `terrain-objectives` — Apply broad environmental rules symmetrically when possible, then let creature traits determine who can exploit them. A common constraint is easier to run and can still create asymmetric tactics. Source: https://slyflourish.com/zone_effects.html
- **TO-008** — `terrain-objectives` — Adjust difficulty by changing access to strengths rather than only changing statistics. Favorable ground and complementary allies intensify a monster's plan; disrupted formation or unsuitable terrain softens it. Source: https://theangrygm.com/the-angry-guide-to-akicking-combats-part-1-picking-your-enemies/

## Source and licensing notes

- Giffyglyph's official FAQ describes Monster Maker as unofficial house rules, permits attributed references in free homebrew, and asks readers not to reproduce it wholesale or include it in a for-sale derivative. This digest therefore retains only independently phrased, nonnumeric design relationships from those pages. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/FAQ.html
- The Blog of Holding summary says its compact statistical reference is offered under CC BY 4.0. This digest nevertheless omits its table values and equations under the stricter clean-room rule for this project. Source: https://www.blogofholding.com/?p=8469

