     5	## Encounter math
     6	
     7	- **EM-001** — `encounter-math` — Treat a difficulty calculation as an early-warning device, not a promise about the result. Use it mainly to detect fights that may be unexpectedly lethal. Source: https://slyflourish.com/5e_encounter_building.html
     8	- **EM-002** — `encounter-math` — Choose opponents that make sense in the fiction before applying a balance check. If the check exposes excessive danger, change the situation or make retreat and failure survivable rather than forcing the fiction to obey a budget. Source: https://slyflourish.com/5e_encounter_building.html
     9	- **EM-003** — `encounter-math` — Calibrate any generic benchmark against the actual group after observing play. Coordination, character options, player experience, equipment, and current resource depletion can all shift practical difficulty. Source: https://slyflourish.com/5e_encounter_building.html
    10	- **EM-004** — `encounter-math` — Evaluate enemy quantity separately from individual rating because additional bodies amplify pressure through targeting, positioning, and extra opportunities to act. A collection of weak foes is not equivalent to a lone creature with the same nominal budget. Source: https://slyflourish.com/5e_encounter_building.html
    11	- **EM-005** — `encounter-math` — Avoid making every battle land on the same difficulty band. Contrasting easy victories, uncertain contests, and clearly overwhelming threats gives the campaign texture and makes danger legible. Source: https://slyflourish.com/5e_encounter_building.html
    12	- **EM-006** — `encounter-math` — When approximate balance may fail, prepare consequences that keep play moving after defeat. Retreat routes, capture, lost objectives, or changed circumstances can make a dangerous result meaningful without ending the campaign. Source: https://slyflourish.com/5e_encounter_building.html
    13	- **EM-007** — `encounter-math` — Judge a creature's threat by how much harm it can inflict before the party neutralizes it, rather than by durability or output alone. Defensive and offensive changes interact multiplicatively because surviving longer creates more chances to deal damage. Source: https://tomedunn.github.io/the-finished-book/monsters/monster-manual-2024/
    14	- **EM-008** — `encounter-math` — Be cautious when inferring a benchmark from sparse high-tier examples. Prefer a broad trend and reserve extreme values for deliberately exceptional designs instead of treating a lone outlier as the new norm. Source: https://www.blogofholding.com/?p=8469
    15	
    16	## Action economy
    17	
    18	- **AE-001** — `action-economy` — Compare how often each side can materially affect the battle, not merely how much damage its best attack deals. A side with more useful turns can reposition, recover from misses, apply control, and finish weakened targets more reliably. Source: https://slyflourish.com/5e_encounter_building.html
    19	- **AE-002** — `action-economy` — Give a solo opponent meaningful activity between player turns so the battlefield responds throughout the round. Off-turn movement, attacks, or control are more engaging than concentrating all of the boss's impact into one turn. Source: https://slyflourish.com/action_oriented_monsters.html
    20	- **AE-003** — `action-economy` — An alternative solo chassis can model several creatures sharing one body. Its available turns and endurance then reflect how many functional components remain, allowing attrition to reduce its battlefield presence. Source: https://theangrygm.com/return-of-the-son-of-the-dd-boss-fight-now-in-5e/
    21	- **AE-004** — `action-economy` — Spread a boss's extra activity across the initiative order instead of granting a single oversized turn. This creates more frequent decision points and reduces long stretches in which the encounter cannot react. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/minions_elites_solos.html
    22	- **AE-005** — `action-economy` — Minions can restore numerical pressure without giving every added body the endurance and complexity of a standard foe. Their job is to consume attention, occupy space, and create openings for more important enemies. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/minions_elites_solos.html
    23	- **AE-006** — `action-economy` — Reinforcements are a flexible difficulty control because they alter enemy activity only if the encounter needs it. Their arrival should follow a visible fictional cause such as an alarm, summons, or nearby guards. Source: https://slyflourish.com/lazy_5e_monster_building_resource_document.html
    24	- **AE-007** — `action-economy` — Faster initiative improves a monster's realized output because acting before opponents can remove or disable it yields more completed actions. Treat initiative capability as part of combat power, especially for elite threats. Source: https://tomedunn.github.io/the-finished-book/monsters/monster-manual-2024/
    25	
    26	## Monster stats
    27	
    28	- **MS-001** — `monster-stats` — Begin with the creature's intended threat and battlefield behavior, then choose statistics that enable that behavior. Starting from a pile of numbers makes it easy to produce a legal but purposeless opponent. Source: https://theangrygm.com/monster-building-202/
    29	- **MS-002** — `monster-stats` — Separate offensive and defensive evaluations so a design can intentionally trade staying power for pressure, or the reverse. The combined result may remain suitable even when the creature has a pronounced strength and weakness. Source: https://theangrygm.com/monster-building-202/
    30	- **MS-003** — `monster-stats` — Count always-on traits and reliable special abilities as part of the relevant stat budget. An effect that routinely improves accuracy, avoidance, durability, or output is not free merely because it is written as prose. Source: https://theangrygm.com/monster-building-202/
    31	- **MS-004** — `monster-stats` — Use baseline statistics as a starting envelope, then tune through play for the specific party. Published creatures at the same nominal threat can vary, and unusual effects are difficult to reduce to a single rating. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/making_monsters.html
    32	- **MS-005** — `monster-stats` — Give each monster dependable ordinary attacks before layering on role-specific powers. A clear baseline keeps the creature functional when its signature move is unavailable and prevents every turn from requiring an exceptional rule. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/making_monsters.html
    33	- **MS-006** — `monster-stats` — Recent monster data suggests accuracy and basic defenses remain comparatively stable while damage growth becomes steeper at upper tiers. Updating older designs therefore calls for special scrutiny of output rather than uniform inflation of every statistic. Source: https://tomedunn.github.io/the-finished-book/monsters/monster-manual-2024/
    34	- **MS-007** — `monster-stats` — For a glass-cannon or tank identity, pair an above-baseline value with a meaningful weakness instead of raising every major statistic together. Data-driven baselines are most useful as a center around which deliberate variation occurs. Source: https://www.blogofholding.com/?p=8469
    35	- **MS-008** — `monster-stats` — Prefer transparent durability over defenses that work only against parties lacking a particular damage solution. Fewer conditional physical resistances make the same creature perform more consistently across groups with different equipment. Source: https://tomedunn.github.io/the-finished-book/monsters/monster-manual-2024/
