    83	## Terrain and objectives
    84	
    85	- **TO-001** — `terrain-objectives` — Define what each side wants before deciding that combat ends with annihilation. Goals such as delay, escape, capture, protection, theft, or interruption create different movement and targeting choices. Source: https://www.themonstersknow.com/thoughts-constructing-encounters/
    86	- **TO-002** — `terrain-objectives` — Make the physical location express the defenders' methods. Choke points, observation lines, concealment, escape routes, and hazards should help inhabitants behave as if they chose or prepared the site. Source: https://www.themonstersknow.com/thoughts-constructing-encounters/
    87	- **TO-003** — `terrain-objectives` — Let terrain carry both tactical and narrative information. Architecture, damage, furnishings, and elevation can explain the site's history while simultaneously shaping cover, routes, and control. Source: https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/794-new-players-guide-how-to-build-tactical-encounters
    88	- **TO-004** — `terrain-objectives` — Include at least one meaningful interaction beyond attacking a creature, such as operating a mechanism, moving an object, rescuing someone, or changing a hazard. The interaction should compete with combat actions strongly enough to create a real choice. Source: https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/794-new-players-guide-how-to-build-tactical-encounters
    89	- **TO-005** — `terrain-objectives` — Design win conditions independently from enemy survival. Once the objective is secured or lost, remaining foes may withdraw, pursue a different aim, or become irrelevant. Source: https://www.dndbeyond.com/posts/794-new-players-guide-how-to-build-tactical-encounters
    90	- **TO-006** — `terrain-objectives` — Use an active threat or deadline to make task completion more important than clearing the map. This keeps an alternative objective from collapsing back into ordinary attrition. Source: https://slyflourish.com/lazy_5e_monster_building_resource_document.html
    91	- **TO-007** — `terrain-objectives` — Apply broad environmental rules symmetrically when possible, then let creature traits determine who can exploit them. A common constraint is easier to run and can still create asymmetric tactics. Source: https://slyflourish.com/zone_effects.html
    92	- **TO-008** — `terrain-objectives` — Adjust difficulty by changing access to strengths rather than only changing statistics. Favorable ground and complementary allies intensify a monster's plan; disrupted formation or unsuitable terrain softens it. Source: https://theangrygm.com/the-angry-guide-to-akicking-combats-part-1-picking-your-enemies/
    93	
    94	## Source and licensing notes
    95	
    96	- Giffyglyph's official FAQ describes Monster Maker as unofficial house rules, permits attributed references in free homebrew, and asks readers not to reproduce it wholesale or include it in a for-sale derivative. This digest therefore retains only independently phrased, nonnumeric design relationships from those pages. Source: https://giffyglyph.com/monstermaker/grimoire/2.1.2/en/FAQ.html
    97	- The Blog of Holding summary says its compact statistical reference is offered under CC BY 4.0. This digest nevertheless omits its table values and equations under the stricter clean-room rule for this project. Source: https://www.blogofholding.com/?p=8469
